\,0.2\\,$ | $\\,0.5871\\,$ | $\\,0.5910\\,$ | $\\,0.5948\\,$ |\n\n*Hint: Note that the interval $-18<X<10$ is symmetric about the mean $\\mu =-4.$*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070321/state.json" "file_sha256" "19d4ab8572713c782a9c02de3c79ad3a210fce60d047a06de2fe9aa05767eaac"} {"question" "q-132312" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, note that the given interval is symmetric about the mean:\n\n$$\nP(-18<X<10)=P(-4-14<X<-4+14)\n$$\n\nSo, we find the cumulative probability using symmetry:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-18<X<10) & =0.1113 \\\\ 2P(-4<X<10) & =0.1113 \\\\ P(-4<X<10) & =0.0557 \\\\ P(X<10)-P(X<-4) & =0.0557 \\\\ P(X<10)-0.5 & =0.0557 \\\\ P(X<10) & =0.5557 \\\\ P(X\\le 10) & =0.5557\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 10) & =0.5557 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{10-(-4)}{\\sigma }) & =0.5557 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{14}{\\sigma }) & =0.5557 \\\\ \\frac{14}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.5557)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(0.14)=0.5557\\,⇒\\,{Φ}^{-1}(0.5557)=0.14.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{14}{\\sigma } & =0.14 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{14}{0.14} \\\\ \\sigma  & =100.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070321/state.json" "file_sha256" "19d4ab8572713c782a9c02de3c79ad3a210fce60d047a06de2fe9aa05767eaac"} {"question" "q-132312" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070321/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a6dddc5a3b492eb5eb76ba9d59ae7c3969142bbe8f43cbe118fc05c50d57b387"} {"question" "q-132312" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070321/state.json" "file_sha256" "19d4ab8572713c782a9c02de3c79ad3a210fce60d047a06de2fe9aa05767eaac"} {"question" "q-132312" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "10"] ["math" "100"] ["math" "20"] ["math" "50"] ["math" "80"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070321/state.json" "file_sha256" "19d4ab8572713c782a9c02de3c79ad3a210fce60d047a06de2fe9aa05767eaac"} {"question" "q-132312" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "100"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070321/state.json" "file_sha256" "19d4ab8572713c782a9c02de3c79ad3a210fce60d047a06de2fe9aa05767eaac"} {"question" "q-132316" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X∼N(\\mu ,{15}^{2})$ is a normal random variable with $P(-5\\mu <X<7\\mu )=0.5762,$ determine $\\mu .$\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.7\\,$ | $\\,0.7580\\,$ | $\\,0.7611\\,$ | $\\,0.7642\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.7881\\,$ | $\\,0.7910\\,$ | $\\,0.7939\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.8159\\,$ | $\\,0.8186\\,$ | $\\,0.8212\\,$ |\n\n*Hint: Note that the interval $-5\\mu <X<7\\mu $ is symmetric about the mean $\\mu .$*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070321/state.json" "file_sha256" "19d4ab8572713c782a9c02de3c79ad3a210fce60d047a06de2fe9aa05767eaac"} {"question" "q-132316" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Note that we can write the given probability as\n\n$$\nP(\\mu -6\\mu <X<\\mu +6\\mu )=0.5762,\n$$\n\nwhich tells us that the interval $-5\\mu <X<7\\mu $ is symmetric about the mean $\\mu .$\n\nFirst, we find the cumulative probability using symmetry:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\mu -6\\mu <X<\\mu +6\\mu ) & =0.5762 \\\\ 2P(\\mu <X<\\mu +6\\mu ) & =0.5762 \\\\ P(\\mu <X<\\mu +6\\mu ) & =0.2881 \\\\ P(X<\\mu +6\\mu )-P(X<\\mu ) & =0.2881 \\\\ P(X<\\mu +6\\mu )-0.5 & =0.2881 \\\\ P(X<\\mu +6\\mu ) & =0.7881 \\\\ P(X\\le \\mu +6\\mu ) & =0.7881 \\\\ P(X\\le 7\\mu ) & =0.7881\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 7\\mu ) & =0.7881 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{7\\mu -\\mu }{15}) & =0.7881 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{6\\mu }{15}) & =0.7881 \\\\ \\frac{6\\mu }{15} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.7881) \\\\ \\frac{2\\mu }{5} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.7881)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(0.8)=0.7881\\,⇒\\,{Φ}^{-1}(0.7881)=0.8.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\mu }{5} & =0.8 \\\\ 2\\mu  & =4 \\\\ \\mu  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070321/state.json" "file_sha256" "19d4ab8572713c782a9c02de3c79ad3a210fce60d047a06de2fe9aa05767eaac"} {"question" "q-132316" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070321/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a6dddc5a3b492eb5eb76ba9d59ae7c3969142bbe8f43cbe118fc05c50d57b387"} {"question" "q-132316" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070321/state.json" "file_sha256" "19d4ab8572713c782a9c02de3c79ad3a210fce60d047a06de2fe9aa05767eaac"} {"question" "q-132316" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "4"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070321/state.json" "file_sha256" "19d4ab8572713c782a9c02de3c79ad3a210fce60d047a06de2fe9aa05767eaac"} {"question" "q-132316" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070321/state.json" "file_sha256" "19d4ab8572713c782a9c02de3c79ad3a210fce60d047a06de2fe9aa05767eaac"} {"question" "q-276252" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X∼N(\\mu ,{7}^{2})$ is a normal random variable with $P(-3\\mu <X<5\\mu )=0.6476,$ use the table to determine $\\mu ,$ rounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.7\\,$ | $\\,0.7611\\,$ | $\\,0.7642\\,$ | $\\,0.7673\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.7910\\,$ | $\\,0.7939\\,$ | $\\,0.7967\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.8186\\,$ | $\\,0.8212\\,$ | $\\,0.8238\\,$ |\n\n*Hint: Note that the interval $-3\\mu <X<5\\mu $ is symmetric about the mean $\\mu .$*\n\n$\\mu ≈$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070321/state.json" "file_sha256" "19d4ab8572713c782a9c02de3c79ad3a210fce60d047a06de2fe9aa05767eaac"} {"question" "q-276252" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Note that we can write the given probability as\n\n$$\nP(\\mu -4\\mu <X<\\mu +4\\mu )=0.6476,\n$$\n\nwhich tells us that the interval $-3\\mu <X<5\\mu $ is symmetric about the mean $\\mu .$\n\nFirst, we find the cumulative probability using symmetry:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\mu -4\\mu <X<\\mu +4\\mu ) & =0.6476 \\\\ 2P(\\mu <X<\\mu +4\\mu ) & =0.6476 \\\\ P(\\mu <X<\\mu +4\\mu ) & =0.3238 \\\\ P(X<\\mu +4\\mu )-P(X<\\mu ) & =0.3238 \\\\ P(X<\\mu +4\\mu )-0.5 & =0.3238 \\\\ P(X<\\mu +4\\mu ) & =0.8238 \\\\ P(X\\le \\mu +4\\mu ) & =0.8238 \\\\ P(X\\le 5\\mu ) & =0.8238\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 5\\mu ) & =0.8238 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{5\\mu -\\mu }{7}) & =0.8238 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{4\\mu }{7}) & =0.8238 \\\\ \\frac{4\\mu }{7} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.8238)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(0.93)=0.8238\\,⇒\\,{Φ}^{-1}(0.8238)=0.93.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4\\mu }{7} & =0.93 \\\\ 4\\mu  & =6.51 \\\\ \\mu  & =\\frac{6.51}{4} \\\\ \\mu  & ≈1.63.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070321/state.json" "file_sha256" "19d4ab8572713c782a9c02de3c79ad3a210fce60d047a06de2fe9aa05767eaac"} {"question" "q-276252" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070321/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a6dddc5a3b492eb5eb76ba9d59ae7c3969142bbe8f43cbe118fc05c50d57b387"} {"question" "q-276252" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070321/state.json" "file_sha256" "19d4ab8572713c782a9c02de3c79ad3a210fce60d047a06de2fe9aa05767eaac"} {"question" "q-276252" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1.63"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070321/state.json" "file_sha256" "19d4ab8572713c782a9c02de3c79ad3a210fce60d047a06de2fe9aa05767eaac"} {"question" "q-276251" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X∼N(3,{\\sigma }^{2})$ is a normal random variable with $P(-1<X<7)=0.8948,$ use the table to find  $\\sigma ,$ rounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,1.5\\,$ | $\\,0.9345\\,$ | $\\,0.9357\\,$ | $\\,0.9370\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.6\\,$ | $\\,0.9463\\,$ | $\\,0.9474\\,$ | $\\,0.9484\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.7\\,$ | $\\,0.9564\\,$ | $\\,0.9573\\,$ | $\\,0.9582\\,$ |\n\n*Hint: Note that the interval $-1<X<7$ is symmetric about the mean $\\mu =3.$*\n\n$\\sigma ≈$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070321/state.json" "file_sha256" "19d4ab8572713c782a9c02de3c79ad3a210fce60d047a06de2fe9aa05767eaac"} {"question" "q-276251" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, note that the given interval is symmetric about the mean:\n\n$$\nP(-1<X<7)=P(3-4<X<3+4)\n$$\n\nSo, we find the cumulative probability using symmetry:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-1<X<7) & =0.8948 \\\\ 2P(3<X<7) & =0.8948 \\\\ P(3<X<7) & =0.4474 \\\\ P(X<7)-P(X<3) & =0.4474 \\\\ P(X<7)-0.5 & =0.4474 \\\\ P(X<7) & =0.9474 \\\\ P(X\\le 7) & =0.9474\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 7) & =0.9474 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{7-3}{\\sigma }) & =0.9474 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{4}{\\sigma }) & =0.9474 \\\\ \\frac{4}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.9474)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(1.62)=0.9474\\,⇒\\,{Φ}^{-1}(0.9474)=1.62.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{\\sigma } & =1.62 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{4}{1.62} \\\\ \\sigma  & ≈2.47.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070321/state.json" "file_sha256" "19d4ab8572713c782a9c02de3c79ad3a210fce60d047a06de2fe9aa05767eaac"} {"question" "q-276251" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070321/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a6dddc5a3b492eb5eb76ba9d59ae7c3969142bbe8f43cbe118fc05c50d57b387"} {"question" "q-276251" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070321/state.json" "file_sha256" "19d4ab8572713c782a9c02de3c79ad3a210fce60d047a06de2fe9aa05767eaac"} {"question" "q-276251" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2.47"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070321/state.json" "file_sha256" "19d4ab8572713c782a9c02de3c79ad3a210fce60d047a06de2fe9aa05767eaac"} {"question" "q-342070" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X∼N(4,{\\sigma }^{2})$ is a normal random variable with $P(2<X<6)=0.2812,$ find ${\\sigma }^{2},$ rounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.3\\,$ | $\\,0.6368\\,$ | $\\,0.6406\\,$ | $\\,0.6443\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.4\\,$ | $\\,0.6736\\,$ | $\\,0.6772\\,$ | $\\,0.6808\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.5\\,$ | $\\,0.7088\\,$ | $\\,0.7123\\,$ | $\\,0.7157\\,$ |\n\n*Hint: Note that the interval $2<X<6$ is symmetric about the mean $\\mu =4.$*\n\n${\\sigma }^{2}≈$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070321/state.json" "file_sha256" "19d4ab8572713c782a9c02de3c79ad3a210fce60d047a06de2fe9aa05767eaac"} {"question" "q-342070" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the cumulative probability using symmetry:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(2<X<6) & =0.2812 \\\\ 2P(4<X<6) & =0.2812 \\\\ P(4<X<6) & =0.1406 \\\\ P(X<6)-P(X<4) & =0.1406 \\\\ P(X<6)-0.5 & =0.1406 \\\\ P(X<6) & =0.6406 \\\\ P(X\\le 6) & =0.6406\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 6) & =0.6406 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{6-4}{\\sigma }) & =0.6406 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{2}{\\sigma }) & =0.6406 \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.6406)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(0.36)=0.6406\\,⇒\\,{Φ}^{-1}(0.6406)=0.36.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sigma } & =0.36 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{2}{0.36} \\\\ \\sigma  & ≈5.555\\,56.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2}=(5.555\\,56)^{2}≈30.86\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070321/state.json" "file_sha256" "19d4ab8572713c782a9c02de3c79ad3a210fce60d047a06de2fe9aa05767eaac"} {"question" "q-342070" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070321/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a6dddc5a3b492eb5eb76ba9d59ae7c3969142bbe8f43cbe118fc05c50d57b387"} {"question" "q-342070" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070321/state.json" "file_sha256" "19d4ab8572713c782a9c02de3c79ad3a210fce60d047a06de2fe9aa05767eaac"} {"question" "q-342070" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "30.86"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070321/state.json" "file_sha256" "19d4ab8572713c782a9c02de3c79ad3a210fce60d047a06de2fe9aa05767eaac"} {"question" "e-14544" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $\\Phi(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X \\sim N(\\mu, 6^2)$ with $P(X > 4) = 0.1841,$ find $\\mu.$\n\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.00 \\,$ | $\\, 0.01 \\,$ | $\\, 0.02 \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\, 0.7 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7580 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7611 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7642 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.8 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7881 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7910 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7939 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.9 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8159 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8186 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8212 \\,$ |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070321/example-14544.json" "file_sha256" "c4eb635fe0b84cf60dc86249e08b3c744e4c7f4b92afb07c35a99928068b10f6"} {"question" "e-14544" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we convert the given probability into a cumulative probability using the complement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 4) & =1-P(X>4) \\\\  & =1-0.1841 \\\\  & =0.8159\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 4) & =0.8159 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{4-\\mu }{6}) & =0.8159 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{4-\\mu }{6}) & =0.8159 \\\\ \\frac{4-\\mu }{6} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.8159)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\Phi(0.90) = 0.8159 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad \\Phi^{-1}( 0.8159) = 0.90.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4-\\mu }{6} & =0.90 \\\\ 4-\\mu  & =5.4 \\\\ \\mu  & =-1.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070321/example-14544.json" "file_sha256" "c4eb635fe0b84cf60dc86249e08b3c744e4c7f4b92afb07c35a99928068b10f6"} {"question" "e-14545" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $\\Phi(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X\\sim N(2.5, \\sigma^2)$ and $P(X \\geq 3) = 0.4013,$ find $\\sigma^2.$\n\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.03 \\,$ | $\\, 0.04 \\,$ | $\\, 0.05 \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\, 0.2 \\,$ | $\\, 0.5910 \\,$ | $\\, 0.5948 \\,$ | $\\, 0.5987 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.3 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6293 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6331 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6368 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.4 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6664 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6700 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6736 \\,$ |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070321/example-14545.json" "file_sha256" "2b63926992c165e793c1bd98eabbf41adce579c9e704644024fa2ee593ca1ee0"} {"question" "e-14545" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we convert the given probability into a cumulative probability using the complement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 3) & =1-P(X\\ge 3) \\\\  & =1-0.4013 \\\\  & =0.5987\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 3) & =0.5987 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{3-2.5}{\\sigma }) & =0.5987 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{0.5}{\\sigma }) & =0.5987 \\\\ \\frac{0.5}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.5987)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\Phi(0.25) = 0.5987 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad \\Phi^{-1}( 0.5987) = 0.25.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{0.5}{\\sigma } & =0.25 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{0.5}{0.25} \\\\ \\sigma  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2}={2}^{2}=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070321/example-14545.json" "file_sha256" "2b63926992c165e793c1bd98eabbf41adce579c9e704644024fa2ee593ca1ee0"} {"question" "e-14546" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $\\Phi(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X \\sim N(0, \\sigma^2)$ is a normal random variable with $P(-3.18 <        X <        3.18) = 0.4038,$ find $\\sigma.$\n\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.02 \\,$ | $\\, 0.03 \\,$ | $\\, 0.04 \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\, 0.4 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6628 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6664 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6700 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.5 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6985 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7019 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7054 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.6 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7324 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7357 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7389 \\,$ |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070321/example-14546.json" "file_sha256" "ba383da1503add407cc2955bffb4602c45b43bad5050252593cb609c34274e61"} {"question" "e-14546" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the cumulative probability using symmetry:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-3.18<X<3.18) & =0.4038 \\\\ 2P(0<X<3.18) & =0.4038 \\\\ P(0<X<3.18) & =0.2019 \\\\ P(X<3.18)-P(X<0) & =0.2019 \\\\ P(X<3.18)-0.5 & =0.2019 \\\\ P(X<3.18) & =0.7019 \\\\ P(X\\le 3.18) & =0.7019\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 3.18) & =0.7019 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{3.18-0}{\\sigma }) & =0.7019 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{3.18}{\\sigma }) & =0.7019 \\\\ \\frac{3.18}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.7019)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\Phi(0.53) = 0.7019 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad \\Phi^{-1}( 0.7019) = 0.53.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3.18}{\\sigma } & =0.53 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{3.18}{0.53} \\\\ \\sigma  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070321/example-14546.json" "file_sha256" "ba383da1503add407cc2955bffb4602c45b43bad5050252593cb609c34274e61"} {"question" "e-14548" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $\\Phi(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X \\sim N(\\mu, 2^2)$ is a normal random variable with $P( -4\\mu<             X <        6\\mu) = 0.6528,$ find $\\mu$ rounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.03 \\,$ | $\\, 0.04 \\,$ | $\\, 0.05 \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\, 0.7 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7673 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7704 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7734 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.8 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7967 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7995 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8023 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.9 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8238 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8264 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8289 \\,$ |\n\n*Hint: Note that the interval $-4\\mu<             X <        6\\mu$ is symmetric about the mean $\\mu.$*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070321/example-14548.json" "file_sha256" "7374ad74d2eca2bdacee4e601804d46471165380d76e92f4be1f8b782523edd9"} {"question" "e-14548" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Note that we can write the given probability as\n\n$$\nP(\\mu -5\\mu <     X <     \\mu +5\\mu) = 0.6528,\n$$\n\nwhich tells us that the interval $-4\\mu<             X <       6\\mu$ is symmetric about the mean $\\mu.$\n\nFirst, we find the cumulative probability using symmetry:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\mu -5\\mu <X<\\mu +5\\mu ) & =0.6528 \\\\ 2P(\\mu <X<\\mu +5\\mu ) & =0.6528 \\\\ P(\\mu <X<\\mu +5\\mu ) & =0.3264 \\\\ P(X<\\mu +5\\mu )-P(X<\\mu ) & =0.3264 \\\\ P(X<\\mu +5\\mu )-0.5 & =0.3264 \\\\ P(X<\\mu +5\\mu ) & =0.8264 \\\\ P(X\\le 6\\mu ) & =0.8264\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 6\\mu ) & =0.8264 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{6\\mu -\\mu }{2}) & =0.8264 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{5\\mu }{2}) & =0.8264 \\\\ \\frac{5\\mu }{2} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.8264)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\Phi(0.94) = 0.8264 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad \\Phi^{-1}( 0.8264) = 0.94.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5\\mu }{2} & =0.94 \\\\ 5\\mu  & =1.88 \\\\ \\mu  & ≈0.38\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070321/example-14548.json" "file_sha256" "7374ad74d2eca2bdacee4e601804d46471165380d76e92f4be1f8b782523edd9"}] "usage" {"e-14545" [] "e-14546" [] "e-14548" [] "e-14544" [] "q-94892" []} "basis" 2470 "retractions" [[17592186111350 :question/problem "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nThe data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X∼N(0, σ^{2})$ is a normal random variable with $P(\\mid X \\mid < 9) = 0.5468$, find $σ^{2}$.\n\n| $z$ | $0.04$ | $0.05$ | $0.06$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0.7$ | $0.7704$ | $0.7734$ | $0.7764$ |\n| $0.8$ | $0.7995$ | $0.8023$ | $0.8051$ |\n| $0.9$ | $0.8264$ | $0.8289$ | $0.8315$ |"] [17592186110263 :question/problem "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $\\Phi(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X \\sim N(\\mu, 6^2)$ with $P(X > 4) = 0.1841$, find $\\mu$.\n\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.00 \\,$ | $\\, 0.01 \\,$ | $\\, 0.02 \\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\, 0.7 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7580 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7611 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7642 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.8 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7881 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7910 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7939 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.9 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8159 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8186 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8212 \\,$ |"] [17592186110263 :question/worked-solution "First, we convert the given probability into a cumulative probability using the complement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X \\le 4) &= 1 - P(X > 4) \\\\\n&= 1 - 0.1841 \\\\\n&= 0.8159\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X \\le 4) &= 0.8159 \\\\\nP(Z \\le \\frac{4 - μ}{6}) &= 0.8159 \\\\\nΦ(\\frac{4 - μ}{6}) &= 0.8159 \\\\\n\\frac{4 - μ}{6} &= Φ^{-1}(0.8159)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\Phi(0.90) = 0.8159 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad \\Phi^{-1}(0.8159) = 0.90\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{4 - μ}{6} &= 0.90 \\\\\n4 - μ &= 5.4 \\\\\nμ &= -1.4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186110264 :question/problem "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $\\Phi(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X\\sim N(2.5, \\sigma^2)$ and $P(X \\geq 3) = 0.4013$, find $\\sigma^2$.\n\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.03 \\,$ | $\\, 0.04 \\,$ | $\\, 0.05 \\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\, 0.2 \\,$ | $\\, 0.5910 \\,$ | $\\, 0.5948 \\,$ | $\\, 0.5987 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.3 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6293 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6331 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6368 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.4 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6664 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6700 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6736 \\,$ |"] [17592186110264 :question/worked-solution "First, we convert the given probability into a cumulative probability using the complement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X \\le 3) &= 1 - P(X \\ge 3) \\\\\n&= 1 - 0.4013 \\\\\n&= 0.5987\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X \\le 3) &= 0.5987 \\\\\nP(Z \\le \\frac{3 - 2.5}{σ}) &= 0.5987 \\\\\nΦ(\\frac{0.5}{σ}) &= 0.5987 \\\\\n\\frac{0.5}{σ} &= Φ^{-1}(0.5987)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\Phi(0.25) = 0.5987 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad \\Phi^{-1}(0.5987) = 0.25\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{0.5}{σ} &= 0.25 \\\\\nσ &= \\frac{0.5}{0.25} \\\\\nσ &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nσ^{2} = 2^{2} = 4\n$$"] [17592186110265 :question/problem "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $\\Phi(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X \\sim N(0, \\sigma^2)$ is a normal random variable with $P(-3.18 < X < 3.18) = 0.4038$, find $\\sigma$.\n\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.02 \\,$ | $\\, 0.03 \\,$ | $\\, 0.04 \\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\, 0.4 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6628 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6664 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6700 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.5 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6985 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7019 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7054 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.6 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7324 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7357 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7389 \\,$ |"] [17592186110265 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the cumulative probability using symmetry:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(-3.18 < X < 3.18) &= 0.4038 \\\\\n2P(0 < X < 3.18) &= 0.4038 \\\\\nP(0 < X < 3.18) &= 0.2019 \\\\\nP(X < 3.18) - P(X < 0) &= 0.2019 \\\\\nP(X < 3.18) - 0.5 &= 0.2019 \\\\\nP(X < 3.18) &= 0.7019 \\\\\nP(X \\le 3.18) &= 0.7019\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X \\le 3.18) &= 0.7019 \\\\\nP(Z \\le \\frac{3.18 - 0}{σ}) &= 0.7019 \\\\\nΦ(\\frac{3.18}{σ}) &= 0.7019 \\\\\n\\frac{3.18}{σ} &= Φ^{-1}(0.7019)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\Phi(0.53) = 0.7019 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad \\Phi^{-1}(0.7019) = 0.53\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{3.18}{σ} &= 0.53 \\\\\nσ &= \\frac{3.18}{0.53} \\\\\nσ &= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186110266 :question/problem "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $\\Phi(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X \\sim N(\\mu, 2^2)$ is a normal random variable with $P(-4\\mu< X < 6\\mu) = 0.6528$, find $\\mu$ rounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.03 \\,$ | $\\, 0.04 \\,$ | $\\, 0.05 \\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\, 0.7 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7673 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7704 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7734 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.8 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7967 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7995 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8023 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.9 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8238 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8264 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8289 \\,$ |\n\n*Hint: Note that the interval $-4\\mu< X < 6\\mu$ is symmetric about the mean $\\mu$.*"] [17592186110266 :question/worked-solution "Note that we can write the given probability as\n\n$$\nP(\\mu -5\\mu < X < \\mu +5\\mu) = 0.6528\n$$\n\nwhich tells us that the interval $-4\\mu< X < 6\\mu$ is symmetric about the mean $\\mu$.\n\nFirst, we find the cumulative probability using symmetry:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(μ - 5μ < X < μ + 5μ) &= 0.6528 \\\\\n2P(μ < X < μ + 5μ) &= 0.6528 \\\\\nP(μ < X < μ + 5μ) &= 0.3264 \\\\\nP(X < μ + 5μ) - P(X < μ) &= 0.3264 \\\\\nP(X < μ + 5μ) - 0.5 &= 0.3264 \\\\\nP(X < μ + 5μ) &= 0.8264 \\\\\nP(X \\le 6μ) &= 0.8264\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X \\le 6μ) &= 0.8264 \\\\\nP(Z \\le \\frac{6μ - μ}{2}) &= 0.8264 \\\\\nΦ(\\frac{5μ}{2}) &= 0.8264 \\\\\n\\frac{5μ}{2} &= Φ^{-1}(0.8264)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\Phi(0.94) = 0.8264 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad \\Phi^{-1}(0.8264) = 0.94\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{5μ}{2} &= 0.94 \\\\\n5μ &= 1.88 \\\\\n&= μ \\mid \\approx 0.38\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186110196 17592186110197 17592186110198 17592186110199 17592186110200] "immutable_fields" [[17592186110195 :answer-field/correct] [17592186110195 :answer-field/id] [17592186110195 :answer-field/key] [17592186110195 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
